mn \\(\\pi(i)\\) to column \\(i\\).  \nAfter these relabellings the uncovered squares become  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal.  \nFrom now on we work with this normalized situation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of the complement  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT^{-}= \\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<r\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nT^{+}= \\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r<c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{-}\\) consists of the cells strictly **below** the diagonal, \\(T^{+}\\) of those strictly **above** it.  \nThe complement of the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nC = T^{-}\\cup T^{+}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle that lies completely in \\(C\\) cannot intersect both \\(T^{-}\\) and \\(T^{+}\\); otherwise, by convexity, it would also contain a diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\), contradicting (2).  \nHence every tile belongs entirely to either \\(T^{-}\\) or \\(T^{+}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound  \n\nConsider the \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 cells immediately adjacent to the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nB^{-}= \\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset T^{-},\\qquad   \nB^{+}= \\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset T^{+}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* A rectangle contained in \\(T^{-}\\) can contain **at most one** element of \\(B^{-}\\).  \n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\subset T^{-}\\) contains two distinct cells \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((j,j-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nBecause \\(R\\) is axis\u2011parallel, it must also contain every cell whose row lies between \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) and whose column lies between \\(i-1\\) and \\(j-1\\).  \nIn particular, it contains the cell \\((i,i)\\), which lies on the diagonal, contradicting the fact that diagonal cells are uncovered. \u220e  \n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) cells of \\(B^{-}\\) are pairwise distinct, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all of \\(T^{-}\\).  \nThe same argument applied to \\(B^{+}