les are disjoint because they occupy distinct rows/columns. They claim they cover all cells not uncovered.\n\nWe need to check if any tile may be empty (if c_i=1 or c_i=n). They count total non-empty rectangles as at most 2n-2. Actually they say total number of non-empty rectangles equals (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. But if some V_i or H_i are empty, the total number of non-empty rectangles could be less than 2n-2. However they claim at most one of V_i and H_i is empty for each i: if c_i=1 then V_i empty; if c_i=n then H_i empty; otherwise both non-empty. So for each i, we have either 2 tiles (if 1<c_i<n) or 1 tile (if c_i=1 or c_i=n). So total number of tiles is (n-1) + (n-1) - (# of i where c_i=1 or c_i=n). Actually they claim total equals (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 regardless of empties. But if some V_i or H_i are empty, then total number of non-empty rectangles is less than 2n-2. But they claim \"total number of non‑empty rectangles equals (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\". This is not accurate if any V_i or H_i are empty. However they also say \"for each i at most one of V_i and H_i is empty; when c_i=1 the vertical tile is empty, when c_i=n the horizontal tile is empty, and in all other cases both are non‑empty.\" So the total number of non‑empty rectangles equals (n-1)+(n-1) minus the number of i where either V_i or H_i is empty? Actually they say \"at most one of V_i and H_i is empty\", meaning each i contributes either 2 or 1 non-empty rectangles. So the total number of non‑empty rectangles is between n-1 (if all i have exactly one tile) and 2(n-1) (if all i have two tiles). They claim it's exactly 2n-2. That would be the case only if for all i, both V_i and H_i are non-empty, i.e., c_i not equal to 1 or n for any i. But c_i is a permutation of {1,...,n}, so there will be exactly one i where c_i=1 and exactly one i where c_i=n. So there will be exactly two indices i where one of the tiles is empty. So total number of non-empty rectangles is (n-1)+(n-1) - 2 = 2n-4. Actually we have n-1 vertical tiles V_i (i=1,...,n-1). Among those, V_i is empty if c_i=1. Since c_i is a permutation, there is exactly one i where c_i=1. So one vertical tile is empty. Similarly, among the n-1 horizontal tiles H_i, one is empty when c_i=n. So we have total non-empty tiles = (n-1)-1 + (n-1)-1 = 2n-4. However, note that V_{n-1} is defined for i up to n-1, but c_{n} is not used for V_i (i up to n-1). The index i where c_i=n could be i=n, but V_i only defined for i up to n-1. Actually V_i uses c_i for i=1,...,n-1. So if c_n=n, that does not affect V_i emptiness. Similarly H_i uses c_i for i=1,...,n-1. So if c_n=n, that does not affect H_i emptiness. Wait, we need to examine more carefully.\n\nThe permutations r_i and c_i are defined for i=1,...,n. The uncovered squares are (r_i, c_i). They reorder rows so r_i increasing. Then they define V_i for i=1,...,n-1 using c_i (the column of uncovered cell in row r_i). If c_i=1, V_i empty. So there is exactly one i among 1,...,n for which c_i=1. That i could be i=n (the last uncovered cell). If c_n=1, then V_i for i=n-1 is not empty because c_{n-1} is something else. So V_i emptiness depends on whether c_i=1 for i=1,...,n-1. If the column 1 appears at i=n, then no V_i is empty. Similarly, H_i emptiness depends on c_i=n for i=1,...,n-1. If column n appears at i=n, then no H_i empty. So it's possible that the empty vertical/horizontal tiles correspond to the uncovered cell at the last row (i=n) being at column 1 or n. Since the permutation is arbitrary, we can arrange that the column 1 and column n appear at i=n (the last row). Then V_i and H_i for i=1,...,n-1 are all non-empty. In that case total number of tiles = 2(n-1) = 2n-2. So the construction can achieve 2n-2 tiles if we choose the permutation appropriately. However, the construction as described does not guarantee that the permutation will have c_n=1 or n. But we can always reorder rows such that r_i increasing; the permutation c_i is arbitrary. We can choose a permutation that places column 1 and column n at the last row (i=n). But does the construction require that? The solution claims \"for each i at most one of V_i and H_i is empty; when c_i=1 the vertical tile is empty, when c_i=n the horizontal tile is empty, and in all other cases both are non‑empty. Hence the total number of non‑empty rectangles equals (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\" This is inaccurate because if c_i=1 for some i < n, then V_i empty, reducing total count. But they claim total equals 2n-2 regardless. So there is a potential issue: The construction may not always produce exactly 2n-2 tiles; it may produce fewer if c_i=1 or n appear earlier. However, we only need to show existence of a tiling with 2n-2 tiles. The solution can choose a specific permutation that yields that number. They haven't explicitly constructed such a permutation, but they claim the construction works for any permutation? Actually they say \"We now exhibit a tiling attaining the bound (5). Recall the ordering (3).\" They define V_i and H_i for each i. They claim \"for each i at most one of V_i and H_i is empty ... total number of non‑empty rectangles equals (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\" This is a mistake: the total number of non-empty rectangles depends on the permutation. So there is a potential error in counting. However, does this affect the validity of the lower bound? The lower bound is 2n-2. If the construction yields fewer tiles, that would contradict the lower bound. But the lower bound is proven using Lemma 1: each tile can cover at most one left neighbor L_i, and there are n-1 left neighbors, so at least n-1 tiles needed. Similarly for right neighbors, at least n-1 tiles needed. However, the same tile could cover both a left neighbor and a right neighbor? The proof assumes they are independent requirements, but is it possible for a single tile to contain both a left neighbor and a right neighbor? They claim \"Applying Lemma 1 to the set R gives another independent requirement of at least n-1 tiles.\" But Lemma 1 says each tile can contain at most one element of L, and also at most one element of R. However, a tile could contain one L and one R simultaneously? The lemma only says at most one L, and at most one R, but does not forbid containing both one L and one R. So the lower bound might be too high: you could have a tile covering one left neighbor and one right neighbor, thus counting both L and R in one tile, reducing total needed. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may be not valid. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have uncovered squares U = {(r_i, c_i)}. L_i = (r_i, c_i-1) if c_i>1. R_i = (r_i, c_i+1) if c_i<n. L = {L_i | i=2,...,n} (i from 2 to n). R = {R_i | i=1,...,n-1}. So there are n-1 left neighbors and n-1 right neighbors. Lemma 1: any rectangle Q contained in complement C can contain at most one element of L. Similarly for R. So each tile can contain at most one L and at most one R. But a tile could contain one L and one R simultaneously, i.e., two cells (one left neighbor, one right neighbor) as long as they are in the same rectangle. However, can a rectangle contain both a left neighbor and a right neighbor? Let's consider a tile covering a region that includes both (r_i, c_i-1) and (r_j, c_j+1) for some i, j. Since L_i and R_j are in different rows possibly, but a rectangle can cover multiple rows and columns. However, the rectangle must be entirely in complement C, i.e., cannot contain any uncovered cell (r_k, c_k). If a rectangle contains both L_i and R_j, it must avoid uncovered cells. Could it happen? Possibly if the rows and columns are arranged such that the rectangle covers a region between uncovered cells. But we need to check if it's possible for a rectangle to contain both a left neighbor and a right neighbor while avoiding uncovered cells. Let's examine typical configuration: Suppose uncovered cells are at positions (r_i, c_i). The left neighbor L_i is directly to the left of uncovered cell i. The right neighbor R_i is directly to the right of uncovered cell i. If a rectangle contains L_i and R_i for the same i, then it must include the uncovered cell (r_i, c_i) because the rectangle's rows include r_i and columns include c_i-1 and c_i+1, so it must also include column c_i (since rectangle includes all columns between c_i-1 and c_i+1). Thus it would contain the uncovered cell, which is not allowed. So a rectangle cannot contain both L_i and R_i for same i. But could it contain L_i and R_j for i != j? Possibly if rows i and j are distinct and the rectangle includes rows from r_i to r_j and columns covering c_i-1 and c_j+1. But then the rectangle would also include the uncovered cells at those rows? Let's think: Suppose we have uncovered cells at (r_i, c_i) and (r_j, c_j). The rectangle includes rows from r_i to r_j (including both) and columns from min(c_i-1, c_j+1) to max(c_i-1, c_j+1). If c_i and c_j are far apart, the rectangle might include uncovered cells (r_i, c_i) and (r_j, c_j). Since the rectangle includes all columns between c_i-1 and c_j+1, it includes column c_i (if c_i-1 < c_i < c_j+1) and column c_j (if c_j+1 > c_j > c_i-1). So it would contain uncovered cells. So a rectangle cannot contain both a left neighbor and a right neighbor for different i unless the rectangle avoids the uncovered cells. But given the geometry, any rectangle that includes a left neighbor L_i (row r_i, column c_i-1) and a right neighbor R_j (row r_j, column c_j+1) will also include the uncovered cells (r_i, c_i) and (r_j, c_j) unless the rectangle's column range excludes those columns. But to include both c_i-1 and c_j+1, the column range must be at least from min(c_i-1, c_j+1) to max(c_i-1, c_j+1). If c_i-1 < c_i < c_j+1, then column c_i is inside the rectangle. So the rectangle would contain uncovered cell (r_i, c_i). Similarly for (r_j, c_j). So it's not possible. However, there might be a scenario where c_i-1 > c_j+1 (i.e., c_i > c_j+2), then the rectangle's column range is from c_j+1 to c_i-1, which does not include c_i or