d by any V_i. However, maybe the construction uses the next uncovered column to the right to cover those cells via H_i? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have a cell (x,y) with y < c_k. The construction says it lies in column c_k-1 and thus belongs to V_k. But if y is less than c_k-1, then column c_k-1 is not equal to y. So the cell is not in column c_k-1. So the claim fails. However, maybe the cell (x,y) would be covered by some V_j for a different j where c_j-1 = y. Indeed, if there is some j such that c_j = y+1, then V_j covers column y for rows between r_j and r_{j+1}. If x lies between those rows, then the cell is covered by V_j. So the coverage argument might rely on the existence of such j. But does such j always exist? Since columns c_i are a permutation of 1..n, for each column y there is exactly one i such that c_i = y+1 (unless y=n). So there is a V_i that covers column y (if y+1 ≤ n). So any column y < n is covered by some V_i. However, the rows covered by V_i are from r_i to r_{i+1} - 1. So a given cell (x,y) may not be in that row interval. But maybe it will be covered by some H_j? Let's examine.\n\nThe set of vertical strips V_i cover all cells left of each uncovered cell, but only in the vertical range between successive uncovered rows. So they partition the region left of each uncovered column into vertical strips aligned with rows between uncovered rows. Similarly, horizontal strips H_i cover cells to the right of each uncovered cell, but only in the horizontal range between successive uncovered columns.\n\nThus the complement can be partitioned into rectangles formed by the \"grid\" defined by the uncovered cells: each rectangle is defined by intervals between successive rows and columns of the permutation matrix. The number of such rectangles is (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. This is a known decomposition: the complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into 2n-2 rectangles, each rectangle being either a vertical strip between two successive rows (cove