angles to tile the board minus a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound 2n-2, and construction achieving that for identity permutation. So answer is 2n-2.\n\nWe need to verify lower bound reasoning: They consider left region: For each row i, squares left of uncovered square (i, pi(i)) are columns 1,..., pi(i)-1. The rightmost covered column in that row is pi(i)-1. They claim the numbers pi(i)-1 run through {0,..., n-1} once each because pi is a permutation. Actually pi(i) runs through {1,...,n} exactly once. So pi(i)-1 runs through {0,..., n-1} exactly once. However note that pi(i)-1 = 0 corresponds to pi(i)=1, meaning there are no squares left of the uncovered square in that row. In that case the rightmost covered column is column 0? Actually there is no column 0; they consider values in {1,..., n-1} for left region? They say \"each distinct value \\pi(i)-1 in {1,..., n-1} must be the rightmost column of a different rectangle.\" But pi(i)-1 can be 0 for rows where uncovered square is in column 1. In those rows, there are no squares left of uncovered square, so they don't need to cover any left region squares. So they consider only values in {1,..., n-1}. Indeed there are n-1 such values (since one row has pi(i)-1 = 0). So they need at least n-1 rectangles for left region.\n\nSimilarly for right region: squares to the right of uncovered squares. For each row i, leftmost covered column in that region is pi(i)+1. The values pi(i)+1 run through {2,..., n+1}? Actually pi(i) runs 1..n, so pi(i)+1 runs 2..n+1. The value n+1 corresponds to pi(i) = n, meaning no squares to the right. So there are n-1 values in {2,..., n} that correspond to rows where there are squares to the right. So need at least n-1 rectangles for right region.\n\nThus lower bound 2n-2.\n\nBut we need to check if the argument that each distinct value must be rightmost column of a different rectangle is valid. They argue: If a rectangle contains two rows i != j, then its rightmost column i