n the “almost diagonal’’ \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n   Define two families of rectangles.\n\n   *Left family* ( \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) )  \n\n   \\[\n   A_k=\\{(i,j):k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(j\\le k<\\pi(i)\\); therefore \\(A_k\\) lies completely to the left of the hole in each of those rows.  \n   The sets \\(A_1,\\dots ,A_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of the left intervals \\(L_i\\).\n\n   *Right family* ( \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) )  \n\n   \\[\n   B_k=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every row \\(i\\le k\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)=i+1\\le k+1<j\\); thus \\(B_k\\) lies completely to the right of the hole in each of those rows.  \n   The sets \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of the right intervals \\(R_i\\).\n\n   The two families are disjoint from each other, and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered cells.  \n   The total number of rectangles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower‑bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the explicit construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\) for the \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered cell be \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), where \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all covered cells is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} R_i .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA rectangle that is placed on the board can intersect the sets\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) in at most one index \\(i\\), and it can intersect the\nsets \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) in at most one index \\(i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a cell of \\(L_i\\) and a cell of\n\\(L_k\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \nLet the right side of \\(T\\) be the vertical line \\(x=c\\) (in column\ncoordinates).  \nBecause the rectangle lies completely to the left of the hole in row\n\\(i\\), we have \\(c<\\pi(i)\\); similarly \\(c<\\pi(k)\\).  \nHence \\(c<\\min\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(k)\\}\\).  \n\nIf \\(T\\) also contained a cell of \\(L_j\\) for some row \\(j\\) with\n\\(\\pi(j)\\le c\\), then that cell would be to the right of the line\n\\(x=c\\), contradicting the definition of \\(c\\).  \nThus \\(T\\) cannot intersect left‑intervals of two different rows.\nThe same argument, applied to the left side of a rectangle, shows that\n\\(T\\) cannot intersect right‑intervals of two different rows. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lower bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the set \\(L_i\\) is non‑empty unless \\(\\pi(i)=1\\);\nthe set \\(R_i\\) is non‑empty unless \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).\nConsequently among the \\(2n\\) sets \\(\\{L_i,R_i\\}\\) at most two are empty\n(the left set of the row whose hole is in column 1 and the right set of\nthe row whose hole is in column \\(n\\)).  \n\nBy Lemma 1 a single rectangle can cover cells belonging to at most one\nnon‑empty left set and at most one non‑empty right set.  \nHence at least  \n\n\\[\n(2n-2)\\qquad\\text{rectangles}\n\\]\n\nare necessary to cover all cells.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=\\begin{cases}\ni+1 & (1\\le i<n),\\\\\n1   & (i=n).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered cells are \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define two rectangles\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_k&=\\{(i,j):k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\