es of the right intervals shows that at least another \\(2024\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).  \n\n   Since a tile cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\), any tiling of the whole board uses at least \\(2024+2024=4048\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.** The lower bound equals the construction, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares.  \nMatilda must place axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, each tile covering a set of unit squares, such that\n\n* each unit square is covered by **at most** one tile,\n* each row and each column contains **exactly one** uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Description of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column has precisely one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\in S_n\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nConsequently column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) also contains exactly that uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition into left and right parts  \n\nFor a fixed \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nEvery tile must lie completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\); a rectangle cannot contain a hole, so it cannot intersect both sides.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=|L_i|=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad r_i:=|R_i|=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); the same holds for \\(\\{r_i\\}\\).\n\n----------------------------------------------------------