------\n#### 3.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) place a horizontal \\(1\\times\\ell_i\\) rectangle covering exactly the squares of \\(L_i\\).  \nSimilarly, for every row \\(i\\) with \\(r_i>0\\) place a horizontal \\(1\\times r_i\\) rectangle covering exactly the squares of \\(R_i\\).\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\) (the row whose hole lies in column 1) and exactly one row has \\(r_i=0\\) (the row whose hole lies in column \\(n\\)).  \nHence the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i\\mid\\ell_i>0\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i\\mid r_i>0\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe prove that no tiling can use fewer tiles.\n\n##### 4.1  The left side  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) let  \n\n\\[\np_i:=(i,\\ell_i)\n\\]\n\nbe the **rightmost** square of that row’s left interval.  \nBecause the numbers \\(\\ell_i\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), the squares \\(\\{p_i\\mid\\ell_i>0\\}\\) are \\(n-1\\) distinct squares of \\(L\\).\n\n**Claim.** Any rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) contains at most one of the squares \\(p_i\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\(I\\) be the set of rows intersected by \\(T\\).  \nDefine \\(i_0\\in I\\) to be a row for which \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\) is minimal among \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is a rectangle, its rightmost column cannot exceed \\(\\ell_{i_0}\\); otherwise column \\(\\ell_{i_0}+1\\) would be present in row \\(i_0\\), contradicting the fact that \\((i_0,\\sigma(i_0))\\) is a hole.  \nConsequently the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is \\(\\le\\ell_{i_0}\\), so \\(T\\) can contain the square \\(p_{i_0}\\) but cannot reach column \\(\\ell_i\\) for any other row \\(i\\in I\\) with \\(\\ell_i>\\ell_{i_0}\\). Hence at most one of the squares \\(p_i\\) li